# Code owners.
#
# These three files ARE the planted-payload control. Whoever can change them without
# review can switch the control off in the same pull request it is meant to inspect —
# the workflow can be kept named `planted-payload-check` while its steps are gutted to
# `run: true`, or pointed at an empty directory so the checker honestly reports
# "inspected 0" and exits 0. A required status check does not catch that: it matches on
# name and outcome, never on content.
#
# IMPORTANT: this file is INERT until `require_code_owner_reviews` is enabled on the
# main branch protection. As of 2026-08-17 it is false on every repo in the fleet, and
# `required_approving_review_count` is 0. Adding the entry is the half that can be done
# in-repo; turning it on is a human decision and has to be made deliberately, because it
# means these paths can no longer be changed by an agent alone.
/.github/workflows/planted-payload-check.yml  @sumitcareteams
/scripts/check_planted_payloads.py            @sumitcareteams
/tests/test_check_planted_payloads.py         @sumitcareteams

# This file owns ITSELF, and that line is load-bearing. GitHub resolves code owners
# from the CODEOWNERS on the PR's BASE branch, so without this entry no owner review
# is required to edit this file - and the bypass is two PRs: the first deletes the
# entries above (nothing matches those paths any more), the second guts the workflow.
# A list of protected paths that is itself unprotected protects nothing.
/.github/CODEOWNERS                           @sumitcareteams
